官方的token account\n"}]},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"浏览器打开平台\n"}]},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"修改价格为0.5usdt\n"}]},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"7MsAkdANC9d9MxRz1G1nndz1F8TnHnWsZBFp2EVQiYE9 确认官方账号是这个钱包地址\n"}]},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"\n# Files mentioned by the user:\n\n## 截屏2026-05-23 17.04.16.png: /var/folders/nd/fkt5ppxs6635vd7rfflhlc7c0000gn/T/TemporaryItems/NSIRD_screencaptureui_WLVScR/截屏2026-05-23 17.04.16.png\n\n## My request for Codex:\n需要在真实solana主网上完成支付\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<image name=[Image #1]>"},{"type":"input_image","image_url":"data:image/png;base64,","detail":"high"},{"type":"input_text","text":"</image>"}]},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"浏览器打开\n"}]},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"GkG9yu2KrtPQzPmdH8BZ1FVaJwM93sBQsabZ2sJbPou\n这个钱包已经有金额，再查询一下\n"}]},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"\n# Files mentioned by the user:\n\n## 截屏2026-05-23 19.02.19.png: /var/folders/nd/fkt5ppxs6635vd7rfflhlc7c0000gn/T/TemporaryItems/NSIRD_screencaptureui_SYs8pW/截屏2026-05-23 19.02.19.png\n\n## My request for Codex:\n这个地址有余额\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<image name=[Image #1]>"},{"type":"input_image","image_url":"data:image/png;base64,","detail":"high"},{"type":"input_text","text":"</image>"}]},{"type":"message","role":"developer","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"<permissions instructions>\nFilesystem sandboxing defines which files can be read or written. `sandbox_mode` is `workspace-write`: The sandbox permits reading files, and editing files in `cwd` and `writable_roots`. Editing files in other directories requires approval. Network access is restricted.\n# Escalation Requests\n\nCommands are run outside the sandbox if they are approved by the user, or match an existing rule that allows it to run unrestricted. The command string is split into independent command segments at shell control operators, including but not limited to:\n\n- Pipes: |\n- Logical operators: &&, ||\n- Command separators: ;\n- Subshell boundaries: (...), $(...)\n\nEach resulting segment is evaluated independently for sandbox restrictions and approval requirements.\n\nExample:\n\ngit pull | tee output.txt\n\nThis is treated as two command segments:\n\n[\"git\", \"pull\"]\n\n[\"tee\", \"output.txt\"]\n\nCommands that use more advanced shell features like redirection (>, >>, <), substitutions ($(...), ...), environment variables (FOO=bar), or wildcard patterns (*, ?) will not be evaluated against rules, to limit the scope of what an approved rule allows.\n\n## How to request escalation\n\nIMPORTANT: To request approval to execute a command that will require escalated privileges:\n\n- Provide the `sandbox_permissions` parameter with the value `\"require_escalated\"`\n- Include a short question asking the user if they want to allow the action in `justification` parameter. e.g. \"Do you want to download and install dependencies for this project?\"\n- Optionally suggest a `prefix_rule` - this will be shown to the user with an option to persist the rule approval for future sessions.\n\nIf you run a command that is important to solving the user's query, but it fails because of sandboxing or with a likely sandbox-related network error (for example DNS/host resolution, registry/index access, or dependency download failure), rerun the command with \"require_escalated\". ALWAYS proceed to use the `justification` parameter - do not message the user before requesting approval for the command.\n\n## When to request escalation\n\nWhile commands are running inside the sandbox, here are some scenarios that will require escalation outside the sandbox:\n\n- You need to run a command that writes to a directory that requires it (e.g. running tests that write to /var)\n- You need to run a GUI app (e.g., open/xdg-open/osascript) to open browsers or files.\n- If you run a command that is important to solving the user's query, but it fails because of sandboxing or with a likely sandbox-related network error (for example DNS/host resolution, registry/index access, or dependency download failure), rerun the command with `require_escalated`. ALWAYS proceed to use the `sandbox_permissions` and `justification` parameters. do not message the user before requesting approval for the command.\n- You are about to take a potentially destructive action such as an `rm` or `git reset` that the user did not explicitly ask for.\n- Be judicious with escalating, but if completing the user's request requires it, you should do so - don't try and circumvent approvals by using other tools.\n\n## prefix_rule guidance\n\nWhen choosing a `prefix_rule`, request one that will allow you to fulfill similar requests from the user in the future without re-requesting escalation. It should be categorical and reasonably scoped to similar capabilities. You should rarely pass the entire command into `prefix_rule`.\n\n### Banned prefix_rules \nAvoid requesting overly broad prefixes that the user would be ill-advised to approve. For example, do not request [\"python3\"], [\"python\", \"-\"], or other similar prefixes that would allow arbitrary scripting.\nNEVER provide a prefix_rule argument for destructive commands like rm.\nNEVER provide a prefix_rule if your command uses a heredoc or herestring. \n\n### Examples\nGood examples of prefixes:\n- [\"npm\", \"run\", \"dev\"]\n- [\"gh\", \"pr\", \"check\"]\n- [\"cargo\", \"test\"]\n\n\n## Approved command prefixes\nThe following prefix rules have already been approved: - [\"gh\"]\n- [\"say\"]\n- [\"kill\"]\n- [\"open\"]\n- [\"/tmp/list-audio-devices\"]\n- [\"/Users/vincet/Library/Android/sdk/emulator/emulator\"]\n- [\"/Users/vincet/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools/adb\"]\n- [\"/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/aigame/tools/set-default-audio-output\"]\n- [\"ps\", \"-Ao\"]\n- [\"ps\", \"-ef\"]\n- [\"curl\", \"-L\"]\n- [\"curl\", \"-s\"]\n- [\"git\", \"add\"]\n- [\"curl\", \"-sL\"]\n- [\"git\", \"push\"]\n- [\"git\", \"clone\"]\n- [\"git\", \"fetch\"]\n- [\"npm\", \"start\"]\n- [\"git\", \"commit\"]\n- [\"xcrun\", \"simctl\"]\n- [\"launchctl\", \"submit\"]\n- [\"killall\", \"coreaudiod\"]\n- [\"node\", \"local-agent-bridge.mjs\"]\n- [\"system_profiler\", \"SPAudioDataType\"]\n- [\"/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/aigame/.venv-moss-tts/bin/moss-tts-nano\", \"generate\"]\n- [\"node\", \"/Users/vincet/Documents/aimusic/codex_recovery/restore_ttgame_codex_threads.mjs\"]\n- [\"node\", \"/Users/vincet/Documents/aimusic/codex_recovery/sync_current_codex_to_ttgame_archive.mjs\"]\n- [\"/Users/vincet/.openclaw/tools/node-v22.22.0/bin/node\", \"/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/aigame/tools/openclaw-local-bridge.mjs\"]\n- [\"say\", \"-a\", \"92\"]\n- [\"afplay\", \"-a\", \"92\"]\n- [\"rm\", \"-rf\", \"Resources\"]\n- [\"python3\", \"-m\", \"http.server\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"say -a '?' 2>&1\"]\n- [\"pkill\", \"-f\", \"scripts/debot-monitor.mjs\"]\n- [\"sed\", \"-n\", \"/players:/,/ducked players/p\"]\n- [\"curl\", \"-s\", \"http://127.0.0.1:18889/health\"]\n- [\"curl\", \"-s\", \"http://127.0.0.1:18900/health\"]\n- [\"curl\", \"-s\", \"http://127.0.0.1:18901/health\"]\n- [\"curl\", \"-s\", \"http://127.0.0.1:18900/enterprise/minimax\"]\n- [\"launchctl\", \"print\", \"system/com.apple.audio.coreaudiod\"]\n- [\"mkdir\", \"-p\", \"/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/aigame/tmp\"]\n- [\"curl\", \"-sL\", \"https://developer.android.com/studio?pkg=studio\"]\n- [\"node\", \"scripts/generate_ios_suno_style_instrumentals.mjs\", \"20\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"ps -ef -ww > /private/tmp/aimuse_ps_chatfix.txt\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"MUSIC_PROXY_HOST=127.0.0.1 node scripts/music_proxy_server.mjs\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"/Users/vincet/Desktop/'Cat Pet.app'/Contents/MacOS/CatPetLauncher\"]\n- [\"curl\", \"-s\", \"http://127.0.0.1:18900/generations/1590d0d2-58a8-4488-aa46-4e514821562c\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"DEBOT_DRY_RUN=1 DEBOT_POLL_MS=600000 node scripts/debot-monitor.mjs\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"find /Users/vincet/Documents -name gradle-wrapper.jar 2>/dev/null | sed -n '1,40p'\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"IOS_MUSIC_BATCH_PREFIX=ios-promptbank-20260507 node scripts/generate_ios_promptbank_via_proxy.mjs 10\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"IOS_MUSIC_BATCH_PREFIX=ios-promptbank-20260507 node scripts/generate_ios_random_instrumentals.mjs 10\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"JAVA_HOME='/Applications/Android Studio.app/Contents/jbr/Contents/Home' ./gradlew assembleDebug --offline\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"/Users/vincet/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools/adb exec-out screencap -p > /tmp/aimuse_wakelock_fixed.png\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"MUSIC_PROXY_HOST=127.0.0.1 nohup node scripts/music_proxy_server.mjs > /private/tmp/aimuse_music_proxy.log 2>&1 &\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"nohup /Users/vincet/Library/Android/sdk/emulator/emulator -avd FlashPlay_API35 > /private/tmp/ipcard-emulator.log 2>&1 &\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"IOS_MUSIC_BATCH_PREFIX=ios-promptbank-20260507 IOS_PROMPTBANK_TARGET_COUNT=10 node scripts/generate_ios_promptbank_one.mjs 3\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"JAVA_HOME='/Applications/Android Studio.app/Contents/jbr/Contents/Home' ./gradlew :app:installDebug --offline --console=plain\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"/Users/vincet/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools/adb -s emulator-5554 exec-out screencap -p > /private/tmp/ipcard-reopened.png\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"JAVA_HOME='/Applications/Android Studio.app/Contents/jbr/Contents/Home' ./gradlew :app:assembleDebug --offline --console=plain\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"/Users/vincet/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools/adb -s emulator-5554 exec-out screencap -p > /private/tmp/ipcard-designers.png\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"/Users/vincet/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools/adb -s emulator-5554 exec-out screencap -p > /private/tmp/ipcard-douyin-feed.png\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"/Users/vincet/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools/adb -s emulator-5554 exec-out screencap -p > /private/tmp/card-feed-first-role.png\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"/Users/vincet/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools/adb -s emulator-5554 exec-out screencap -p > /private/tmp/ipcard-designers-top.png\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"/Users/vincet/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools/adb -s emulator-5554 exec-out screencap -p > /private/tmp/ipcard-douyin-detail.png\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"/Users/vincet/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools/adb -s emulator-5554 exec-out screencap -p > /private/tmp/ipcard-imagegen-feed.png\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"/Users/vincet/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools/adb -s emulator-5554 exec-out screencap -p > /private/tmp/ipcard-purchase-flow.png\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"/Users/vincet/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools/adb -s emulator-5554 exec-out screencap -p > /private/tmp/ipcard-scope-collected.png\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"/Users/vincet/Lib...\n[Some commands were truncated]\n\n`approvals_reviewer` is `auto_review`: Sandbox escalations with require_escalated will be reviewed for compliance with the policy. If a rejection happens, you should proceed only with a materially safer alternative, or inform the user of the risk and send a final message to ask for approval.\n The writable roots are `/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass`, `/private/tmp`, `/private/var/folders/nd/fkt5ppxs6635vd7rfflhlc7c0000gn/T`.\n</permissions instructions>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<app-context>\n# Codex desktop context\n- You are running inside the Codex (desktop) app, which allows some additional features not available in the CLI alone:\n\n### Images/Visuals/Files\n- In the app, the model can display images and videos using standard Markdown image syntax: ![alt](url)\n- When sending or referencing a local image or video, always use an absolute filesystem path in the Markdown image tag (e.g., ![alt](/absolute/path.png)); relative paths and plain text will not render the media.\n- When referencing code or workspace files in responses, always use full absolute file paths instead of relative paths.\n- If a user asks about an image, or asks you to create an image, it is often a good idea to show the image to them in your response.\n- Use mermaid diagrams to represent complex diagrams, graphs, or workflows. Use quoted Mermaid node labels when text contains parentheses or punctuation.\n- Return web URLs as Markdown links (e.g., [label](https://example.com)).\n\n### Workspace Dependencies\n- For sheets, slides, and documents, call `load_workspace_dependencies` to find the bundled runtime and libraries.\n\n### Automations\n- This app supports recurring automations, reminders, monitors, follow-ups, and thread wakeups. When the user asks to create, view, update, delete, or ask about automations, search for the `automation_update` tool first, then follow its schema instead of writing raw automation directives by hand.\n\n### Inline Code Comments\n- Use the ::code-comment{...} directive when you need to attach feedback directly to specific code lines.\n- Emit one directive per inline comment; emit none when there are no actionable inline comments.\n- Required attributes: title (short label), body (one-paragraph explanation), file (path to the file).\n- Optional attributes: start, end (1-based line numbers), priority (0-3).\n- file should be an absolute path or include the workspace folder segment so it can be resolved relative to the workspace.\n- Keep line ranges tight; end defaults to start.\n- Example: ::code-comment{title=\"[P2] Off-by-one\" body=\"Loop iterates past the end when length is 0.\" file=\"/path/to/foo.ts\" start=10 end=11 priority=2}\n\n### Archiving\n- If a user specifically asks you to end a thread/conversation, you can return the archive directive ::archive{...} to archive the thread/conversation.\n- Example: ::archive{reason=\"User requested to end conversation\"}\n</app-context>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"## Memory\n\nYou have access to a memory folder with guidance from prior runs. It can save\ntime and help you stay consistent. Use it whenever it is likely to help.\n\nDecision boundary: should you use memory for a new user query?\n\n- Skip memory ONLY when the request is clearly self-contained and does not need\n  workspace history, conventions, or prior decisions.\n- Hard skip examples: current time/date, simple translation, simple sentence\n  rewrite, one-line shell command, trivial formatting.\n- Use memory by default when ANY of these are true:\n  - the query mentions workspace/repo/module/path/files in MEMORY_SUMMARY below,\n  - the user asks for prior context / consistency / previous decisions,\n  - the task is ambiguous and could depend on earlier project choices,\n  - the ask is a non-trivial and related to MEMORY_SUMMARY below.\n- If unsure, do a quick memory pass.\n\nMemory layout (general -> specific):\n\n- /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/memory_summary.md (already provided below; do NOT open again)\n- /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md (searchable registry; primary file to query)\n- /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/skills/<skill-name>/ (skill folder)\n  - SKILL.md (entrypoint instructions)\n  - scripts/ (optional helper scripts)\n  - examples/ (optional example outputs)\n  - templates/ (optional templates)\n - /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ (per-rollout recaps + evidence snippets)\n  - The paths of these entries can be found in /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md or /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ as `rollout_path`\n  - These files are append-only `jsonl`: `session_meta.payload.id` identifies the session, `turn_context` marks turn boundaries, `event_msg` is the lightweight status stream, and `response_item` contains actual messages, tool calls, and tool outputs.\n  - For efficient lookup, prefer matching the filename suffix or `session_meta.payload.id`; avoid broad full-content scans unless needed.\n\nQuick memory pass (when applicable):\n\n1. Skim the MEMORY_SUMMARY below and extract task-relevant keywords.\n2. Search /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md using those keywords.\n3. Only if MEMORY.md directly points to rollout summaries/skills, open the 1-2\n   most relevant files under /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ or\n   /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/skills/.\n4. If above are not clear and you need exact commands, error text, or precise evidence, search over `rollout_path` for more evidence.\n5. If there are no relevant hits, stop memory lookup and continue normally.\n\nQuick-pass budget:\n\n- Keep memory lookup lightweight: ideally <= 4-6 search steps before main work.\n- Avoid broad scans of all rollout summaries.\n\nDuring execution: if you hit repeated errors, confusing behavior, or suspect\nrelevant prior context, redo the quick memory pass.\n\nHow to decide whether to verify memory:\n\n- Consider both risk of drift and verification effort.\n- If a fact is likely to drift and is cheap to verify, verify it before\n  answering.\n- If a fact is likely to drift but verification is expensive, slow, or\n  disruptive, it is acceptable to answer from memory in an interactive turn,\n  but you should say that it is memory-derived, note that it may be stale, and\n  consider offering to refresh it live.\n- If a fact is lower-drift and cheap to verify, use judgment: verification is\n  more important when the fact is central to the answer or especially easy to\n  confirm.\n- If a fact is lower-drift and expensive to verify, it is usually fine to\n  answer from memory directly.\n\nWhen answering from memory without current verification:\n\n- If you rely on memory for a fact that you did not verify in the current turn,\n  say so briefly in the final answer.\n- If that fact is plausibly drift-prone or comes from an older note, older\n  snapshot, or prior run summary, say that it may be stale or outdated.\n- If live verification was skipped and a refresh would be useful in the\n  interactive context, consider offering to verify or refresh it live.\n- Do not present unverified memory-derived facts as confirmed-current.\n- For interactive requests, prefer a short refresh offer over silently doing\n  expensive verification that the user did not ask for.\n- When the unverified fact is about prior results, commands, timing, or an\n  older snapshot, a concrete refresh offer can be especially helpful.\n\nMemory citation requirements:\n\n- If ANY relevant memory files were used: append exactly one\n`<oai-mem-citation>` block as the VERY LAST content of the final reply.\n  Normal responses should include the answer first, then append the\n`<oai-mem-citation>` block at the end.\n- Use this exact structure for programmatic parsing:\n```\n<oai-mem-citation>\n<citation_entries>\nMEMORY.md:234-236|note=[responsesapi citation extraction code pointer]\nrollout_summaries/2026-02-17T21-23-02-LN3m-weekly_memory_report_pivot_from_git_history.md:10-12|note=[weekly report format]\n</citation_entries>\n<rollout_ids>\n019c6e27-e55b-73d1-87d8-4e01f1f75043\n019c7714-3b77-74d1-9866-e1f484aae2ab\n</rollout_ids>\n</oai-mem-citation>\n```\n- `citation_entries` is for rendering:\n  - one citation entry per line\n  - format: `<file>:<line_start>-<line_end>|note=[<how memory was used>]`\n  - use file paths relative to the memory base path (for example, `MEMORY.md`,\n    `rollout_summaries/...`, `skills/...`)\n  - only cite files actually used under the memory base path (do not cite\n    workspace files as memory citations)\n  - if you used `MEMORY.md` and then a rollout summary/skill file, cite both\n  - list entries in order of importance (most important first)\n  - `note` should be short, single-line, and use simple characters only (avoid\n    unusual symbols, no newlines)\n- `rollout_ids` is for us to track what previous rollouts you find useful:\n  - include one rollout id per line\n  - rollout ids should look like UUIDs (for example,\n    `019c6e27-e55b-73d1-87d8-4e01f1f75043`)\n  - include unique ids only; do not repeat ids\n  - an empty `<rollout_ids>` section is allowed if no rollout ids are available\n  - you can find rollout ids in rollout summary files and MEMORY.md\n  - do not include file paths or notes in this section\n  - For every `citation_entries`, try to find and cite the corresponding rollout id if possible\n- Never include memory citations inside pull-request messages.\n- Never cite blank lines; double-check ranges.\n\nUpdating memories:\n\nYou can update the memories **only** when explicitly asked by the user. This must always come from a direct request from the user.\n- Write your update in /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/extensions/ad_hoc/notes/\n- Each update must be one small file containing what you want to add/delete/update from the memories.\n- The name of this file must be `<timestamp>-<short slug>.md`\n- Do not try to edit the memory files yourself, only add one update note in /Users/vincet/.codex/memories/extensions/ad_hoc/notes/\n\n========= MEMORY_SUMMARY BEGINS =========\n## User Profile\n\nThe user works across several local prototypes at once and treats Codex, Godot debug windows, iPhone Simulator, Chrome, Finder, Telegram, and desktop app windows as one working surface. They often work in Chinese, but preserve exact English file paths, UI copy, commands, error strings, and product names when those are the real routing handles. [chronicle memory]\n\nThe highest-activity current scopes are `/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/aigame-dock`, `/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/maze-treasure-thinker`, `/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/godot-2d-treasure`, `/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/aigame`, `/Users/vincet/Documents/skills/codex-memory-backup`, and local Codex automation/plugin surfaces. Recent Chronicle context adds that the user is using Codex desktop itself as a planning and automation surface, including session-history inspection, Gmail-backed automations, and a recurring UI-design automation for an AI game platform concept. [chronicle memory]\n\nFor Godot and web-game-distribution work, the user repeatedly validates against live runtime behavior instead of trusting code summaries. Current high-signal game threads include `Maze Treasure Thinker` chapter/story compression plus tileset/runtime validation, `Quiet Dungeon Treasure` gameplay/AI/collection systems, `Quiet Dungeon Treasure Demo` publishing, and a newer May 22-23 push into `AIGame Dock` storefront implementation, real web-demo embedding, demo/full-version split planning, and product-UI simplification. [chronicle memory]\n\nFor mobile app work, the user expects simulator-visible proof. The surviving FlashPlay memory is about DeepSeek-backed generation and account-deletion/reset verification from the `Me` tab rather than from backend claims alone. [chronicle memory]\n\nThe user preserves exact handles and dislikes lossy abstraction. Good collaboration means keeping project paths, filenames, bundle ids, config keys, automation names, thread titles, model names, ports, and visible UI copy intact, then proving the result in the live surface that actually matters for that task. [chronicle memory]\n\n## User preferences\n\n- Use the live surface as truth: Godot debug windows, iPhone Simulator, Chrome dashboards/pages, Finder artifact views, and Codex automation panels outrank an unverified code-only claim. [chronicle memory]\n- Preserve exact routing handles first: project paths, filenames, commands, error strings, bundle ids, config keys, automation names, thread titles, model names, and visible UI copy. [chronicle memory]\n- In Godot work, move quickly from plan to a runnable debug window; do not stop at ideation if the next step can be a playable prototype. [chronicle memory]\n- For `/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/godot-2d-treasure`, keep early versions small when the request is “a dungeon treasure-hunting game” and “not add dungeon enemies,” then grow systems only after the loop is playable. [chronicle memory]\n- Once a playable Godot loop exists, separate gameplay changes from visual-skin changes; if the request is “pure exploration, coin/gem collection” plus a palette/art swap, do not redesign the loop. [chronicle memory]\n- In `godot-2d-treasure` tuning passes, expect concrete live corrections like teammate spacing, enemy non-attacks, chapter-card button placement, chest-opening rules, attack-lock behavior, or persistent projectile clutter; keep the game running and fix the exact interaction. [chronicle memory]\n- After combat/input changes in `godot-2d-treasure`, keep manual playtesting until live outcomes such as `Attack missed.`, `Frozen`, `You collapsed.`, `Healing fountain restored +1 HP`, `Reef stalker defeated.`, or visible bubble/fireball behavior appear. [chronicle memory]\n- When Chapter 2/3/4 work is in play, preserve chapter-specific logic and art instead of flattening everything into one biome or combat rule set. [chronicle memory]\n- When chapter-menu polish is requested in `Quiet Dungeon Treasure`, keep `Challenge` and `AI Challenge` inside each chapter card, reuse existing chapter assets, keep the work menu-only, and visually inspect the live `Choose Chapter` screen after each pass. [chronicle memory]\n- When AI Challenge is involved, prefer real-time state/strategy iteration over fixed autoplay. Make takeover/fallback state explicit and keep the JSON/state/log files visible. [chronicle memory]\n- When AI Challenge is restored after test-heavy work, keep the main AI character as `player`, add `assist_player` only as a helper, and keep camera/exit/AI state anchored to `player`. [chronicle memory]\n- When packaging `Quiet Dungeon Treasure` for Android, “done” means a signed APK plus visible delivery or install path, not just changed export settings. [chronicle memory]\n- When redesigning `Quiet Dungeon Treasure` rewards or collection UI, keep collectibles separate from bag capacity, preserve the chapter-themed `3 x 4` shelf model, and verify icon/nameplate/chest-glow alignment in the live debug window. [chronicle memory]\n- When planning the maze-treasure variant, first use generated/reference art to establish the material direction, then implement/test feature by feature instead of jumping straight from idea to code. [chronicle memory]\n- In `maze-treasure-thinker`, when player guidance is requested “without giving away puzzle answers,” default to short HUD/objective prompts that clarify the next step or risk tradeoff instead of solution spoilers. [chronicle memory]\n- When a maze-treasure automation finishes a new chapter, include a summary email with screenshot/content instead of reporting only that chapter logic changed. [chronicle memory]\n- For Codex desktop history or automation work, preserve literal automation names, skill handles, and thread titles rather than replacing them with generic summaries. [chronicle memory]\n- For the hourly AI video-editing scan, keep the dedupe memory file in the loop, separate independent products from platform-feature updates, and only describe delivery as sent via Gmail when a concrete send record exists.\n- For `/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/aigame`, “backend fixed” still means the booted simulator shows the expected app state or generated game after relaunch. Keep `backend/.env`, provider wiring, and simulator state tied together. [chronicle memory]\n- In FlashPlay account-deletion/reset work, verify the visible `Generated` and `Favorites` lists after the action and explicitly separate account-scoped cleanup from settings that intentionally remain local, such as language or device id. [chronicle memory]\n- When a publishing platform blocks account creation with hCaptcha or VPN/proxy checks, pivot to the next workable distribution surface instead of stalling on abstract platform advice. [chronicle memory]\n- For `Quiet Dungeon Treasure Demo` publishing, keep the indie-hosting page as the demo/community surface and keep paid/full-version handling separate. Default to draft/restricted visibility until builds, cover, and screenshots are ready. [chronicle memory]\n- When packaging the demo, keep the first release constrained: first chapter only, downloadable demo zips, and a clear split between demo/public page and full-version monetization. [chronicle memory]\n- When product/UI discussion becomes too platform-heavy, simplify toward a lightweight `try first, then one-click buy full version` flow instead of a complex Web3/storefront/admin surface. [chronicle memory]\n- For recurring UI-design help, keep outputs in the same thread and require all key interaction states, not just a homepage concept. [chronicle memory]\n- When reviewing storefront mockups, keep only the core end-user states in view: discovery, demo play/resume, payment, download, and `My Games` re-download/update; reduce detail-page recommendations and admin-style clutter. [chronicle memory]\n- For `/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/aigame-dock`, keep the local Chrome preview on `127.0.0.1:5179` as the truth surface and verify the real `Play Demo` / `Resume Demo` / `Restart Demo View` / `Buy Full Game $8` / download flow rather than stopping at a static mockup. [chronicle memory]\n- In `aigame-dock`, keep `No account needed for demo` and `Sign in only at checkout` explicit; gate wallet/download only around the paid full build. [chronicle memory]\n- When replacing storefront hero art for `Quiet Dungeon Treasure`, prefer composites built from actual game assets over generic concept art, and if the image does not change visually, check served `app.js`/asset paths, cache-busting, and the local server directory before assuming the asset generation failed. [chronicle memory]\n- For wallet/NFT flows, keep the trust boundary explicit: the agent may prepare or explain, but the wallet confirms the final transaction; reduce player friction and keep non-wallet players playable. [chronicle memory]\n\n## General Tips\n\n- Use the visible live surface as the first verification target. [chronicle memory]\n- Preserve exact filenames, project paths, commands, ports, error strings, contract strings, and config keys. [chronicle memory]\n- For `/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/godot-2d-treasure`, check `README.md`, `assets/ASSET_PROVENANCE.md`, `project.godot`, `scenes/main.tscn`, and especially `scripts/main.gd` first. [chronicle memory]\n- For current `Quiet Dungeon Treasure` AI Challenge work, grep `/private/tmp/quiet_dungeon_treasure_ai_state.json`, `/private/tmp/quiet_dungeon_treasure_ai_command.json`, `/private/tmp/quiet_dungeon_treasure_ai_challenge_log.json`, `/private/tmp/quiet_dungeon_treasure_ai_strategy_log.json`, and `tools/codex_ai_supervisor.mjs` first. [chronicle memory]\n- For `maze-treasure-thinker`, start with `/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/tools/godot4`, `tools/generate_playable_maps.py`, `tools/refine_playable8_map_edges.py`, `tools/apply_imagen_edge_tileset.py`, `assets/generated/playable8_maps`, and `reports/screenshots/tilesetter--chapter*.png`. [chronicle memory]\n- For Codex desktop history visibility, start with `/Users/vinc…1229 tokens truncated…e later Node.js/backend/webhook unlock architecture for paid access. [chronicle memory]\n  - learnings: the durable rule is demo/community on the hosting page, payment and unlock elsewhere. Keep checkout external, let the backend own `paid` state, and treat Godot unlock data as a cache. [chronicle memory]\n\n### workflow:ai-game-platform-ui-automation\n\n#### 2026-05-22\n\n- `AI游戏平台UI功能设计优化` automation, `AIGame Dock`, and lightweight game-download UI framing: `AI游戏平台UI功能设计优化`, `ai-ui`, `AIGame Dock`, `Clockwork Island`, `GPT-5.2`, `every 1 hour`, `same thread`, `Play Demo`, `Buy Full Game`, `Download`, `My Games`\n  - desc: Search here for the May 22 Codex-side product/UI planning around a wallet-login game download platform: short/mid/long-term product framing, simplification away from heavy Web3/storefront UX, the recurring automation that generates/refines the interaction designs, and the later `AIGame Dock` mockup QA pass. [chronicle memory]\n  - learnings: the user wanted one thread, hourly cadence, and five interaction-state screens rather than a single homepage concept. The later mockup review reinforced the same rule with concrete screens: keep `Clockwork Island`-style discover/demo/pay/download states, keep `My Games` focused on owned titles and updates, and avoid extra detail-page clutter. [chronicle memory]\n\n### /Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/maze-treasure-thinker\n\n#### 2026-05-21\n\n- Maze Treasure Thinker chapter compression, Tilesetter Lite install, and 8-chapter story/menu integration: `Stone`, `Echo`, `Void`, `Balance`, `Resonance`, `Lattice`, `Nexus`, `Concord`, `Tilesetter Lite.app`, `selected-8-chapters-story-menu.png`, `8 curated chapter callbacks`\n  - desc: Search here first for `cwd=/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/maze-treasure-thinker` work that compressed the maze concept into 8 differentiated chapters, installed `Tilesetter Lite`, and wired the chapter-story/menu direction into the Godot project. [chronicle memory]\n  - learnings: the durable handles are the 8 chapter names, the local Tilesetter install path, and the story/menu checkpoint around `selected-8-chapters-story-menu.png`. [chronicle memory]\n\n- Maze Treasure Thinker playable-map edge refinement and Stone Maze guidance: `tools/refine_playable8_map_edges.py`, `tools/apply_imagen_edge_tileset.py`, `oriented_wall_tile`, `vertical-wall-chapter18-resonance.png`, `Chapter 1 - Stone Maze`, `Next: Find the Stone Key`\n  - desc: Use this topic for the May 21 visual-polish plus player-guidance loop in the same checkout: stale Godot import/cache output, playable-map edge refinement, Imagen wall-material follow-up, and the later non-spoiler HUD/objective prompt plan for Stone Maze. [chronicle memory]\n  - learnings: two important triggers recur here. Godot cache state can hide the latest map PNGs, and player guidance should become short `Next:` HUD prompts that update after key/risk state changes without spoiling solutions. [chronicle memory]\n\n### workflow:codex-desktop-session-history\n\n#### 2026-05-21\n\n- Codex folder thread visibility and global session storage: `本文件夹其他线程记录为什么不显示`, `/Users/vincet/.codex/sessions`, `/Users/vincet/.codex/archived_sessions`, `/Users/vincet/.codex/session_index.jsonl`, `/Users/vincet/.codex/logs_2.sqlite`, `/Users/vincet/.codex/state_5.sqlite`\n  - desc: Search here for May 21 Codex desktop support context: why other thread records were not visible for the current folder, how the workspace/folder view differs from global session storage, and which local `.codex` files hold the truth on this machine. [chronicle memory]\n  - learnings: the key distinction is folder filtering versus global session storage; pausing an automation does not delete thread history. [chronicle memory]\n\n### Older Memory Topics\n\n#### /Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/godot-2d-treasure\n\n- Quiet Dungeon Treasure maze redesign and Codex asset-generation follow-up: `制定新AI游戏方案`, `Chapter 1: Stone Maze`, `Path Mouse`, `assets/maze_treasure/`, `Maze Eye`, `Wall Mimic`, `tools/slice_maze_imagen_assets.py`\n  - desc: Covers the May 18 redesign/generation branch in `cwd=/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/godot-2d-treasure`: the shift toward a `Stone Maze` MVP, separate maze assets, and later generated-file/output routing for the maze-treasure variant.\n\n- Quiet Dungeon Treasure combat, collection, companion, and AI Challenge systems: `slime_tribe`, `Protect chest!`, `monster_attack_skill`, `Chapter Complete`, `Forest Collection`, `Volcano Collection`, `assist_player`, `moon_swordswoman`, `Choose Chapter`\n  - desc: Covers the dense May 16-18 gameplay/system work in `cwd=/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/godot-2d-treasure`: slime evolution, chest/exit guard AI, later-chapter attack skills, collection routing, companion rewards/shop drafting, chapter-card polish, AI Challenge state/supervisor work, and helper-character conversion.\n\n- Quiet Dungeon Treasure Android export and late Chapter 3/4 validation: `QuietDungeonTreasure-debug.apk`, `export_presets.cfg`, `debug.keystore`, `Reef stalker`, `Ember spirit`, `MENU`, `You Collapsed`\n  - desc: Use this topic for export/packaging and older late-prototype validation: Android APK export and Telegram handoff, plus Chapter 3/4 runtime fixes around projectiles, collapse/retry, follower spacing, and chapter return flow in `cwd=/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/godot-2d-treasure`.\n\n- Quiet Dungeon Treasure treasure bag, collection shelf, and wallet/NFT framing: `Treasure Bag`, `Slime Collection 8 / 12`, `Slime Heart`, `ERC-1155`, `avoid paid NFT gating`\n  - desc: Covers the collection/bag rules, shelf-layout normalization, and optional wallet/NFT framing that should not break normal gameplay.\n\n#### /Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/aigame\n\n- FlashPlay DeepSeek provider wiring and simulator validation: `com.flashplay.demo`, `Cat Catch`, `Doggy Ball Chase`, `providerOrder: deepseek`, `deepseek.configured: true`, `backend/.env`, `backend/src/providers/deepseek-provider.mjs`\n  - desc: Search here first for exact `cwd=/Users/vincet/Documents/aiglass/aigame` work on local DeepSeek-backed generation, backend/provider wiring, and simulator-visible validation after relaunch.\n\n#### /Users/vincet/Documents/skills\n\n- Hourly AI video editing tools scan, dedupe memory, and Chinese Gmail delivery: `hourly-ai-video-editing-tools-scan`, `/Users/vincet/.codex/automations/hourly-ai-video-editing-tools-scan/memory.md`, `每小时视频剪辑AI新工具简报`, `_send_email`, `message id 19e4ad54d463638c`\n  - desc: Search here first for the recurring hourly automation that scans for new AI video editing tools, dedupes against local memory, sends a concise Chinese Gmail brief, and appends the final report state back into the automation memory.\n\n#### /Users/vincet/Documents/skills/codex-memory-backup\n\n- Codex account backup sync, strict 90MB split cap, and local-vs-remote push truth: `split -b 89000000`, `SHA256SUMS`, `codex-account-memory-20260519-203238`, `9df788e1b8a89960b47b1782f70acdca6da4b93d`, `main...origin/main [ahead 1]`, `e5c8278a904eb5142385e8a925a56554fe6704b4`\n  - desc: Search here first for `cwd=/Users/vincet/Documents/skills/codex-memory-backup` automation work: exact archive include/exclude rules, verify-before-delete replacement of `memory/` artifacts, the strict decimal split-size rule, and the distinction between local verification and confirmed push success.\n\n#### workflow:flashplay-ios-account-deletion-verification\n\n- FlashPlay account deletion and guest reset: `Me`, `Account deletion`, `Continue as Guest`, `Generated`, `Favorites`, `Cat Jump`, `device id`\n  - desc: Covers surviving May 16 `Me`-tab verification of guest-vs-signed-in state, Firestore-backed account-deletion/reset behavior, and which list contents/settings should or should not survive reset in Simulator. [chronicle memory]\n\n#### workflow:codex-automation-gmail\n\n- Codex Gmail connector, hourly video report, and chapter-summary email follow-up: `Hourly AI video editing tools scan`, `Gmail`, `SENT`, `GPT-5.2`, `Maze Treasure chapter expansion`, `Codex memory backup sync`\n  - desc: Use this topic for Codex desktop automation/plugin setup, Gmail-backed report delivery, exact automation inventory/status, and the follow-up request to include chapter-summary email plus screenshot/content.\n\n#### workflow:ai-startup-product-research\n\n- `自建新大模型` and AI startup/product direction research: `at least 10 items`, `what it does`, `why it is important`, `commercial value`, `sources`, `local deployment`, `model-agnostic`\n  - desc: Covers the May 18 research threads that were separate from coding work: recent AI startup/product directions and enterprise-agent/company-profile criteria.\n\n#### workflow:codexmint-eth-nft-planning\n\n- CodexMint direct-image NFT page and game-collectible wallet UX: `wallet confirms`, `pixel-lobster-original.png`, `ERC-1155`, `embedded wallet`, `avoid paid NFT gating`\n  - desc: Search here for the direct-image `Pixel Lobster Matrix` page boundary plus the later optional game-collectible wallet/NFT framing.\n\n#### workflow:onenode-wallet-prototype\n\n- OneNode `Wallet Guard` plus standalone email-wallet demo: `127.0.0.1:8790`, `Wallet Guard`, `email-wallet-demo.html`, `email-wallet-local-server.mjs`, `ew_session`\n  - desc: Covers the wallet-native agent preview, marketplace-informed wallet/onboarding research, the standalone `email-wallet-demo.html`, and the later loopback session server.\n\n#### workflow:ai-skill-stack-research\n\n- Codex workspace skill catalog, automations, and stuck-push status: `skill-agent`, `Hourly AI startup scan`, `Codex 3D建模学习`, `查找本地 Codex 线程历史`, `git-remote-https`, `pack-objects`, `code-review-pro`\n  - desc: Use this topic when the user wants exact Codex workspace handles rather than generic advice: installed skill names, active automations, thread-history lookup, and the diagnosis boundary for a long-running local `git push`.\n========= MEMORY_SUMMARY ENDS =========\n\nWhen memory is likely relevant, start with the quick memory pass above before\ndeep repo exploration.\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<collaboration_mode># Collaboration Mode: Default\n\nYou are now in Default mode. Any previous instructions for other modes (e.g. Plan mode) are no longer active.\n\nYour active mode changes only when new developer instructions with a different `<collaboration_mode>...</collaboration_mode>` change it; user requests or tool descriptions do not change mode by themselves. Known mode names are Default and Plan.\n\n## request_user_input availability\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only when it is listed in the available tools for this turn.\n\nIn Default mode, strongly prefer making reasonable assumptions and executing the user's request rather than stopping to ask questions. If you absolutely must ask a question because the answer cannot be discovered from local context and a reasonable assumption would be risky, ask the user directly with a concise plain-text question. Never write a multiple choice question as a textual assistant message.\n</collaboration_mode>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<apps_instructions>\n## Apps (Connectors)\nApps (Connectors) can be explicitly triggered in user messages in the format `[$app-name](app://{connector_id})`. Apps can also be implicitly triggered as long as the context suggests usage of available apps.\nAn app is equivalent to a set of MCP tools within the `codex_apps` MCP.\nAn installed app's MCP tools are either provided to you already, or can be lazy-loaded through the `tool_search` tool. If `tool_search` is available, the apps that are searchable by `tools_search` will be listed by it.\nDo not additionally call list_mcp_resources or list_mcp_resource_templates for apps.\n</apps_instructions>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<skills_instructions>\n## Skills\nA skill is a set of local instructions to follow that is stored in a `SKILL.md` file. Below is the list of skills that can be used. Each entry includes a name, description, and a short path that can be expanded into an absolute path using the skill roots table.\n### Skill roots\n- `r0` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/skills`\n- `r1` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/skills/.system`\n- `r2` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled`\n- `r3` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/build-ios-apps/6188456f/skills`\n- `r4` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/build-macos-apps/6188456f/skills`\n- `r5` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/build-web-apps/6188456f/skills`\n- `r6` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/circleci/6188456f/skills`\n- `r7` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/cloudflare/6188456f/skills`\n- `r8` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated`\n- `r9` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/game-studio/6188456f/skills`\n- `r10` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/github/6188456f/skills`\n- `r11` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/gmail/6188456f/skills`\n- `r12` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/stripe/6188456f/skills`\n- `r13` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/test-android-apps/6188456f/skills`\n- `r14` = `/Users/vincet/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-primary-runtime`\n### Available skills\n- imagegen: Generate or edit raster images when the task benefits from AI-created bitmap visuals such as photos, illustrations, textures, sprites, mockups, or transparent-background cutouts. Use when Codex should create a brand-new image, tra (file: r1/imagegen/SKILL.md)\n- openai-docs: Use when the user asks how to build with OpenAI products or APIs and needs up-to-date official documentation with citations, help choosing the latest model for a use case, or model upgrade and prompt-upgrade guidance; prioritize Ope (file: r1/openai-docs/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-creator: Create and scaffold plugin directories for Codex with a required `.codex-plugin/plugin.json`, optional plugin folders/files, and baseline placeholders you can edit before publishing or testing. Use when Codex needs to create a new (file: r1/plugin-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-creator: Guide for creating effective skills. This skill should be used when users want to create a new skill (or update an existing skill) that extends Codex's capabilities with specialized knowledge, workflows, or tool integrations. (file: r1/skill-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-installer: Install Codex skills into $CODEX_HOME/skills from a curated list or a GitHub repo path. Use when a user asks to list installable skills, install a curated skill, or install a skill from another repo (including private repos). (file: r1/skill-installer/SKILL.md)\n- browser:browser: Browser automation for the Codex in-app browser. Use to open, navigate, inspect, test, click, type, screenshot, or verify local targets such as localhost, 127.0.0.1, ::1, file://, the current in-app browser tab, and websites sho (file: r2/browser/0.1.0-alpha2/skills/browser/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:ios-app-intents: Design and implement App Intents, app entities, and App Shortcuts for iOS apps so useful actions and content are available to Shortcuts, Siri, Spotlight, widgets, controls, and other intent-driven system surfaces. Use when exposi (file: r3/ios-app-intents/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:ios-debugger-agent: Use XcodeBuildMCP to build, run, launch, and debug the current iOS project on a booted simulator. Trigger when asked to run an iOS app, interact with the simulator UI, inspect on-screen state, capture logs/console output, or diagno (file: r3/ios-debugger-agent/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:ios-ettrace-performance: Capture and interpret ETTrace profiles for iOS simulator apps, including symbolicated launch and runtime flamegraphs. Use when asked to profile an iOS app flow, gather simulator performance traces, identify CPU-heavy stacks, comp (file: r3/ios-ettrace-performance/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:ios-memgraph-leaks: Capture, inspect, compare, and root-cause iOS memory graph leaks using Apple's leaks and memgraph tools. Use when debugging leaked iOS objects, simulator memgraphs, retain-cycle suspicions, memory growth after navigation/logout/acc (file: r3/ios-memgraph-leaks/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:swiftui-liquid-glass: Implement, review, or improve SwiftUI features using the iOS 26+ Liquid Glass API. Use when asked to adopt Liquid Glass in new SwiftUI UI, refactor an existing feature to Liquid Glass, or review Liquid Glass usage for correctness,  (file: r3/swiftui-liquid-glass/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:swiftui-performance-audit: Audit and improve SwiftUI runtime performance from code review and architecture. Use for requests to diagnose slow rendering, janky scrolling, high CPU/memory usage, excessive view updates, or layout thrash in SwiftUI apps, and t (file: r3/swiftui-performance-audit/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:swiftui-ui-patterns: Best practices and example-driven guidance for building SwiftUI views and components, including navigation hierarchies, custom view modifiers, and responsive layouts with stacks and grids. Use when creating or refactoring SwiftUI (file: r3/swiftui-ui-patterns/SKILL.md)\n- build-ios-apps:swiftui-view-refactor: Refactor and review SwiftUI view files with strong defaults for small dedicated subviews, MV-over-MVVM data flow, stable view trees, explicit dependency injection, and correct Observation usage. Use when cleaning up a SwiftUI vie (file: r3/swiftui-view-refactor/SKILL.md)\n- build-macos-apps:appkit-interop: Decide when and how to bridge a macOS app from SwiftUI into AppKit. Use when implementing NSViewRepresentable or NSViewControllerRepresentable, accessing NSWindow or the responder chain, presenting panels, customizing menus, or h (file: r4/appkit-interop/SKILL.md)\n- build-macos-apps:build-run-debug: Build, run, and debug local macOS apps and desktop executables using shell-first Xcode and Swift workflows. Use when asked to build a Mac app, launch it, diagnose compiler or linker failures, inspect startup problems, or debug desk (file: r4/build-run-debug/SKILL.md)\n- build-macos-apps:liquid-glass: Implement, refactor, or review modern macOS SwiftUI UI for the new design system and Liquid Glass. Use when adopting Liquid Glass, updating NavigationSplitView, toolbars, search, sheets, and controls, removing custom backgrounds  (file: r4/liquid-glass/SKILL.md)\n- build-macos-apps:packaging-notarization: Prepare and troubleshoot packaging, signing, and notarization workflows for macOS distribution. Use when asked to archive a Mac app, validate bundle structure, reason about notarization readiness, or explain distribution-only fai (file: r4/packaging-nota